Rem ==================================================================
Rem sparse.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: sparse matrices
Rem
Rem A Sparse is column-major: the Rows and Cols counts, plus two
Rem parallel outer arrays indexed by column — ColIx, whose every entry
Rem is an Array Of Number of 1-based row indices in ascending order,
Rem and ColVs, the matching values. Nothing else is kept: a zero is an
Rem absence, and an empty column is a pair of empty arrays.
Rem
Rem The public surface:
Rem
Rem   SpFromEnts(r, c, es)     build from SpEnt triplets; duplicates at
Rem                            one (row, col) are summed and a sum of
Rem                            zero is dropped
Rem   SpFromCols(r, ixs, vss)  build from ready-made columns
Rem   SpFromMat / SpToMat      the dense bridges, exact both ways
Rem   SpGet, SpNnz             read a cell, count what is stored
Rem   SpColIx, SpColVs         a column's indices and values, o(1)
Rem   SpColDot(s, j, y)        y . a_j, the pricing kernel
Rem   SpMatVec(s, x)           A x        SpVecMat(y, s)   y' A
Rem   SpTransp(s)              the row view, for the one caller that
Rem                            wants rows out of a column-major store
Rem   SpAddCol, SpAddRow       grow one
Rem   SpShow(s)                display (the one effect; call from Main)
Rem
Rem Why column-major. The revised simplex consumes columns — pricing
Rem wants a_j, FTRAN wants a_e — and Shoddy's arrays are persistent by
Rem cloning, so appending a column copies the outer spine of references
Rem and shares every inner array untouched. That is the one genuinely
Rem cheap way to grow a matrix here, and it is the one the solver and
Rem the branch & bound layer above it need.
Rem
Rem This machine owns the structure only, on the matrix/lin precedent.
Rem matrix.shoddy is untouched and stays the dense, everyday grid;
Rem simplex.shoddy is the algorithm over this one. Sparse pays only
Rem where the zeros dominate and the dimensions are big enough for the
Rem interpreter to feel them — for a small dense grid, Matrix is
Rem cheaper and clearer, and it is what you should reach for.
Rem
Rem Refusals are at construction: a column whose indices and values
Rem do not pair up, or run outside the stated row count, or do not
Rem ascend, is refused there rather than answered wrongly later.
Rem
Rem Pure throughout apart from SpShow: every word answers a new value.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "matrix.shoddy"

Type Sparse
    Rows  As Number
    Cols  As Number
    ColIx As Array Of Array Of Number
    ColVs As Array Of Array Of Number

Rem The interchange form: a numeric, index-keyed triplet. Deliberately
Rem not mps.shoddy's MpsEnt, which is name-keyed — turning names into
Rem indices is exactly the assembly step between the two.

Type SpEnt
    EntRow As Number
    EntCol As Number
    EntVal As Number

Rem ---- refusing a malformed column ------------------------------------

Def SpAscends(ix As Array Of Number) As Boolean
    All(Range(2, Length(ix)), Fn(k) => Nth(ix, k) > Nth(ix, k - 1))

Def SpWithin(ix As Array Of Number, n As Number) As Boolean
    All(ix, Fn(i) => i >= 1 And i <= n)

Rem Answers the indices it was given, so the check sits on the path
Rem that builds the column rather than beside it.

Def SpOkCol(ix As Array Of Number, vs As Array Of Number, n As Number) As Array Of Number
    If Length(ix) <> Length(vs) Then
        Error("SPARSE: INDEX AND VALUE COUNTS DIFFER")
    Else
        If Not SpWithin(ix, n) Then
            Error("SPARSE: INDEX OUT OF RANGE")
        Else
            If Not SpAscends(ix) Then
                Error("SPARSE: INDICES NOT ASCENDING")
            Else
                ix

Rem ---- construction ----------------------------------------------------

Def SpFromCols(r As Number, ixs As List, vss As List) As Sparse
    If Length(ixs) <> Length(vss) Then
        Error("SPARSE: COLUMN COUNTS DIFFER")
    Else
        Sparse(r, Length(ixs),
               ToArray(Map(Range(1, Length(ixs)), Fn(j) => SpOkCol(Nth(ixs, j), Nth(vss, j), r))),
               ToArray(vss))

Def SpEntOk(e As SpEnt, r As Number, c As Number) As SpEnt
    If EntRow(e) < 1 Or EntRow(e) > r Or EntCol(e) < 1 Or EntCol(e) > c Then
        Error("SPARSE: ENTRY OUTSIDE THE STATED DIMENSIONS")
    Else
        e

Rem One column's entries, kept ascending as they arrive. A repeat of a
Rem row index adds to what is already there, left to right, which is
Rem the same order (and so the same last bit) a dense assembly summing
Rem the entry list would have reached.

Def SpIns(ps As List, i As Number, v As Number) As List
    If IsEmpty(ps) Then
        { Pair(i, v) }
    Else
        Select Case Sgn(i - Fst(First(ps)))
            Case -1
                Prepend(Pair(i, v), ps)
            Case 0
                Prepend(Pair(i, Snd(First(ps)) + v), Rest(ps))
            Case Else
                Prepend(First(ps), SpIns(Rest(ps), i, v))

Def SpBucket(bs As Array Of List, e As SpEnt) As Array Of List
    SetNth(bs, EntCol(e), SpIns(Nth(bs, EntCol(e)), EntRow(e), EntVal(e)))

Def SpFromEnts(r As Number, c As Number, es As List) As Sparse
    Let bs = Fold(Map(es, Fn(e) => SpEntOk(e, r, c)), Dim(c, { }), SpBucket)
    Let ks = Map(ToList(bs), Fn(ps) => Filter(ps, Fn(p) => Snd(p) <> 0))
    SpFromCols(r, Map(ks, Fn(ps) => ToArray(Map(ps, Fst))),
                  Map(ks, Fn(ps) => ToArray(Map(ps, Snd))))

Rem ---- the dense bridges ------------------------------------------------

Def SpMatColIx(m As Matrix, c As Number) As List
    Filter(Range(1, Rows(m)), Fn(r) => MatGet(m, r, c) <> 0)

Def SpFromMat(m As Matrix) As Sparse
    Let ixs = Map(Range(1, Cols(m)), Fn(c) => SpMatColIx(m, c))
    SpFromCols(Rows(m), Map(ixs, ToArray),
               Map(Range(1, Cols(m)), Fn(c) => ToArray(Map(Nth(ixs, c), Fn(r) => MatGet(m, r, c)))))

Rem ---- reading ---------------------------------------------------------

Def SpColIx(s As Sparse, j As Number) As Array Of Number
    Nth(ColIx(s), j)

Def SpColVs(s As Sparse, j As Number) As Array Of Number
    Nth(ColVs(s), j)

Def SpNnz(s As Sparse) As Number
    Fold(ColIx(s), 0, Fn(a, ix) => a + Length(ix))

Rem The position of row r in an ascending index array, 0 for absent —
Rem and absent is decided the moment the indices step past r.

Def SpFind(ix As Array Of Number, r As Number, k As Number) As Number
    If k > Length(ix) Then
        0
    Else
        Select Case Sgn(Nth(ix, k) - r)
            Case 0
                k
            Case 1
                0
            Case Else
                SpFind(ix, r, k + 1)

Def SpGet(s As Sparse, r As Number, c As Number) As Number
    Let k = SpFind(SpColIx(s, c), r, 1)
    If k = 0 Then
        0
    Else
        Nth(SpColVs(s, c), k)

Rem ---- arithmetic -------------------------------------------------------

Rem Ascending through the stored entries, which is the same order a
Rem dense dot would take with the zeros left in — and a zero term
Rem changes no bit of the running sum, so this answers what the dense
Rem product answers, to the last place.

Def SpDotAt(ix As Array Of Number, vs As Array Of Number, y As Array Of Number, k As Number, acc As Number) As Number
    If k > Length(ix) Then
        acc
    Else
        SpDotAt(ix, vs, y, k + 1, acc + Nth(y, Nth(ix, k)) * Nth(vs, k))

Def SpColDot(s As Sparse, j As Number, y As Array Of Number) As Number
    SpDotAt(SpColIx(s, j), SpColVs(s, j), y, 1, 0)

Def SpVecMat(y As Array Of Number, s As Sparse) As Array Of Number
    If Length(y) <> Rows(s) Then
        Error("SPVECMAT: DIMENSION MISMATCH")
    Else
        ToArray(Map(Range(1, Cols(s)), Fn(j) => SpColDot(s, j, y)))

Def SpEntsOf(ix As Array Of Number, vs As Array Of Number, j As Number) As List
    Map(Range(1, Length(ix)), Fn(k) => SpEnt(Nth(ix, k), j, Nth(vs, k)))

Def SpColEnts(s As Sparse, j As Number) As List
    SpEntsOf(SpColIx(s, j), SpColVs(s, j), j)

Def SpEnts(s As Sparse) As List
    Flatten(Map(Range(1, Cols(s)), Fn(j) => SpColEnts(s, j)))

Def SpTransp(s As Sparse) As Sparse
    SpFromEnts(Cols(s), Rows(s), Map(SpEnts(s), Fn(e) => SpEnt(EntCol(e), EntRow(e), EntVal(e))))

Rem A x, by taking the row view once and dotting x against each row.

Def SpMatVec(s As Sparse, x As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    If Length(x) <> Cols(s) Then
        Error("SPMATVEC: DIMENSION MISMATCH")
    Else
        SpVecMat(x, SpTransp(s))

Rem ---- back to a dense Matrix ------------------------------------------

Def SpDense(ix As Array Of Number, vs As Array Of Number, n As Number) As Array Of Number
    Fold(Range(1, Length(ix)), Dim(n, 0), Fn(a, k) => SetNth(a, Nth(ix, k), Nth(vs, k)))

Def SpToMat(s As Sparse) As Matrix
    If Rows(s) = 0 Then
        Matrix(0, Cols(s), Dim(0, 0))
    Else
        Let t = SpTransp(s)
        MatFromRows(Map(Range(1, Rows(s)), Fn(i) => SpDense(SpColIx(t, i), SpColVs(t, i), Cols(s))))

Rem ---- growing one ------------------------------------------------------

Rem A new column clones the two outer spines and shares every inner
Rem array, which is what makes appending cheap.

Def SpAddCol(s As Sparse, ix As Array Of Number, vs As Array Of Number) As Sparse
    Sparse(Rows(s), Cols(s) + 1,
           Concat(ColIx(s), Dim(1, SpOkCol(ix, vs, Rows(s)))),
           Concat(ColVs(s), Dim(1, vs)))

Def SpPutEnd(s As Sparse, j As Number, i As Number, v As Number) As Sparse
    With(s, ColIx = SetNth(ColIx(s), j, Concat(SpColIx(s, j), Dim(1, i))),
            ColVs = SetNth(ColVs(s), j, Concat(SpColVs(s, j), Dim(1, v))))

Def SpPutCell(s As Sparse, j As Number, i As Number, v As Number) As Sparse
    If v = 0 Then
        s
    Else
        SpPutEnd(s, j, i, v)

Rem A new row is the last row, so every entry lands at the end of its
Rem column and the ascending order holds without a search. Only the
Rem columns named are touched — for a branch or bound row, exactly one.

Def SpAddRow(s As Sparse, ix As Array Of Number, vs As Array Of Number) As Sparse
    Let js = SpOkCol(ix, vs, Cols(s))
    Let i = Rows(s) + 1
    Let grown = Fold(Range(1, Length(js)), s, Fn(a, k) => SpPutCell(a, Nth(js, k), i, Nth(vs, k)))
    With(grown, Rows = i)

Rem ---- display (effectful; call from Main) -----------------------------

Def SpColTxt(ix As Array Of Number, vs As Array Of Number) As String
    Fold(Range(1, Length(ix)), "", Fn(a, k) => a & " " & Str(Nth(ix, k)) & ":" & Str(Nth(vs, k)))

Def SpShowCol(s As Sparse, j As Number)
    Print("  COL " & Str(j) & SpColTxt(SpColIx(s, j), SpColVs(s, j)))

Rem Column by column, because that is how it is stored — a row-by-row
Rem picture of a sparse matrix is mostly zeros that are not there.

Def SpShow(s As Sparse)
    Print("SPARSE " & Str(Rows(s)) & " X " & Str(Cols(s)) & ", " & Str(SpNnz(s)) & " STORED")
    Each(Range(1, Cols(s)), Fn(j) => SpShowCol(s, j))
